-- world.lth: the world as a token threaded through actions, actions described over the library's
-- representation of the world, externs as routes validated at their first action, and the parts of
-- the world a program touches.
--
-- Draft 1, 2026-10-13, by Claude Fable 5.1 in the session (step 6.1 of
-- record/plan/luthier-from-zero/plan-from-zero-2026-10-13-execution.md, before evidence by
-- D-2026-10-13-ad); draft 0 at 1f8c61c, rewritten on the internal review of layers 2 and 3
-- (record/IA/opus-2026-10-13/review-layers-2-3-report.md, world.1 to world.9). 2026-10-15, by Claude
-- Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/certification-2026-10-14/
-- prompt-first-action.md: `Host` a theory with its laws (§5), `appendStr` given a body (§4).
-- 2026-10-15, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/
-- layer-3-review-2026-10-15/prompt-fix-host.md (the external review of layer 3, round 1, its
-- summary.md): `Host` over the `Console` model, its laws predicates over an observed case,
-- `agreement` the action that gathers the case, the host's side of 10.S7 and the root of trust
-- (W1, W2); the declared type in `Route`, the laws over provisional routes built as such,
-- `RecordCrossesByField` over a field anywhere in a record, `OwnIsIdempotent` with the result,
-- `settled` at the demand's cutoff (W3 to W8); `readFile` at the standard output's place (M1).
-- Sources: spec 10.S1
-- to 10.S13; L11; L4.5; D4 and D8 of record/plan/archive/plan-implementation-2026-10-13-decisions.md
-- (the token used exactly once; `W` optional in `main`); D-2026-10-13-a (one world; an action is a
-- directed request, checked by comparing the representation before and after), -b (`W` a
-- keyword), -g; D-2026-10-14-a (functions cross the bridge as callbacks, counted); D-2026-10-14-c
-- (every cutoff a law needs is a parameter the demand supplies). Pattern of ebnf.lth: a theory,
-- its laws over a model, `T := laws`.
--
-- What this file states. The core knows of the world only the table of 10.S5: the token `W`, a
-- keyword with no `==` and no introduction, which a program never constructs and uses exactly once
-- (D4); and `own`, whether an action concerns Luthier's own store. An action is a function that
-- takes the token first and gives the token after with its result, `W -> ... -> W * A` (10.S3; D8;
-- 2.S17's `w1 <- print w0 "x"`). Since a token is used once and nothing generates one, no law can
-- apply an action to a token twice or compare what two tokens read (the review's world.1): the laws
-- are stated over the library's representation of the world, a type `S` with structural equality,
-- and over an action's description, a function `S -> S * A` that says what the action does to the
-- representation. Those laws are tested by sampling `S`. The host's action is then certified
-- against the console's description along one chain of tokens: observe, act once, observe again
-- (D-2026-10-13-a); that protocol is `agreement`, an action (10.S3, 12.S23) that threads the
-- token and gathers the case, and `Host`'s laws are predicates over the case, which the runner
-- evaluates at an action's first application in a run (10.S10). What cannot be tested (the disk
-- persists while the program is off; the observer reads the world) is assumed (10.S13, 6.S10).
--
-- Index. Status of each section: done, or todo with what it needs. A session or Codex continuing
-- this file takes the first todo, writes it in the pattern of the done ones, checks it with
-- `luthier parse src/luthier/lib/world.lth`, sets its status to done here, and commits.
--
--   §1 The token, the representation, actions and their descriptions; own actions idempotent
--      (10.S3 to 10.S6; D4, D8).                                                      done
--   §2 Places: what is read at one; an action changes only the places it declares, at every
--      other place; what is assumed (10.S7, 10.S13).                                   done
--   §3 Externs as routes: read at the first demand, the hash of what was read in the
--      identity, validated against the type's laws at the demand's cutoff before applied,
--      not applied when the type states no law, a provisional one applied as debt without
--      validation (10.S8 to 10.S10, L11.2, L11.3, 4.R5); a Luthier function crossing as a
--      callback, its calls counted, a record of functions crossing by field (3.S23;
--      D-2026-10-14-a, 12.S18).                                                        done
--   §4 The actions the first slice needs, by their descriptions: `print`, reading a file,
--      observing; the console's descriptions the host is held to (10.S2; D8).          done
--   §5 Certifying a host action against the console's description along one chain of
--      tokens: `agreement`, the action that gathers the case (D-2026-10-13-a); `Host` a
--      theory over the `Console` model whose laws are predicates over the case: each
--      action agrees with its description, no place not declared changes, the observation
--      reads what is there and changes nothing; the observer's fidelity an assumption
--      (10.S7, 10.S10, 10.S13, L11.2).                                                 done
--   §6 Named worlds: an extern with no law runs in one, priced as an assumption (10.S12,
--      L11.6). Waits for evidence (certification.lth).                                 todo
--   §7 The externs of sonolocalización (numpy and its data formats). Waits for the
--      inventory, record/IA/sonolocalizacion-inventory-2026-10-13/.                     todo

import "kernel_laws.lth" Concrete
import "term.lth" Term
import "test.lth" Grade AssumedGrade gradeRank

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 The token, the representation, actions

-- An action takes the token first and gives the token after with its result (10.S3, 10.S4, D8):
-- its type says it is an action, and a function that mentions no `W` holds no effect (10.S1).
Action (A : U) = W -> W * A

-- The library's representation of the world: a type `S` with structural equality, so that two
-- states can be compared (D-2026-10-13-a: an action is checked by comparing the representation
-- before and after). What is read at a place of it is `read`; the places are any string (a file
-- path, "stdout", "clock"), so an action that writes where nothing was declared is seen (the
-- review's world.6).
World (S : Concrete) : { read : S -> String -> Maybe String }

-- An action's description: what it does to the representation, a function `S -> S * A`, with
-- whether it concerns Luthier's own store (10.S6, `own`, from the declaration and not from the
-- effect; what declares nothing is a world action, the safer reading) and the places it declares
-- it changes or reads (10.S7; what `agreement` observes). The token is used exactly once (D4): an action whose new token nobody uses is
-- not demanded; that is the checker's over blocks (program.lth), not a law here.
Described (S : Concrete) (A : U) (m : World S) : {
  describe : S -> S * A,
  own : Bool,
  places : List String,
}
stateAfter (S : Concrete) (A : U) (p : S * A) : S := match p { (s, x) -> s }
resultOf (S : Concrete) (A : U) (p : S * A) : A := match p { (s, x) -> x }

-- An own action is idempotent and memoizable (10.S6): describing it on its own result gives the
-- same state and the same result, the pair a memo serves (the review's W6: the state alone let a
-- memoized result differ from a second run's). `d` is the model under certification, so `d.own`
-- is its declaration and not a draw: the law is vacuous on a world action and tested on every own
-- one (the review's W4, as Fable reads it).
OwnIsIdempotent (S : Concrete) (A : U) (m : World S) (d : Described S A m) (s : S) : Bool :=
  not d.own or d.describe (stateAfter S A (d.describe s)) == d.describe s

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 Places and what an action changes

-- An action may change the world and Luthier's store, and nothing else (10.S7): not a definition,
-- not a binding of another block. In the world it changes only the places it declares: at any other
-- place, what is read after is what was read before. Quantified over every place, so a random path
-- is one the action must leave alone, which is the case to test.
memberStr (x : String) (xs : List String) : Bool := match xs { nil -> false ; cons y rest -> x == y or memberStr x rest }
ChangesOnlyDeclared (S : Concrete) (A : U) (m : World S) (d : Described S A m) (s : S) (p : String) : Bool :=
  memberStr p d.places or m.read (stateAfter S A (d.describe s)) p == m.read s p

Described (S : Concrete) (A : U) (m : World S) := OwnIsIdempotent S A m & ChangesOnlyDeclared S A m

-- What cannot be tested is assumed, so that the certificate of a run says under what hypotheses it
-- holds (10.S13, 6.S10): what is written at a place is read back at it, and a write changes no
-- other place (the disk persists). Stated as laws of the representation; their evidence for the
-- host is a citation or an assumption (laws.lth, `Assumes`), never a sample.
Persistence (S : Concrete) : {
  world : World S,
  write : S -> String -> String -> S,
}
WriteThenRead (S : Concrete) (s : Persistence S) (st : S) (p c : String) : Bool :=
  s.world.read (s.write st p c) p == just c
WriteChangesOnlyItsPlace (S : Concrete) (s : Persistence S) (st : S) (p c q : String) : Bool :=
  p == q or s.world.read (s.write st p c) q == s.world.read st q
Persistence (S : Concrete) := WriteThenRead S & WriteChangesOnlyItsPlace S

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 Externs as routes

-- An extern is a name, a declared type and a route to a host function: data (3.S40, 10.S8;
-- D-2026-10-12-ac: a definition like any other, not an action). The declared type is the core's
-- term after elaboration (term.lth), what the checker records with the target (4.S24); the laws
-- it states are those of the theory it names (6.S1), and they are what the extern is validated
-- against (the review's W3: without the type in the route, "not applied without laws" could not
-- be said). Its file is read when a demand reaches the node, once per plan, and may be absent
-- while the program is checked (L11.7); the hash of the bytes read, computed by the core, enters
-- the identity of what the extern produces and of its certificate (10.S9). `hash` is the core's
-- (spec 6.S8), declared here until core names it.
hash : String -> String
Route = { name : String, declared : Term, route : String, provisional : Bool }
routeOf (n : String) (t : Term) (route : String) (provisional : Bool) : Route :=
  { name := n, declared := t, route := route, provisional := provisional }

-- The first time an action asks for an extern, before it is applied, it is tested against the laws
-- of its declared type like any function, and refused if it fails (10.S10, L11.2). The verdict is
-- of the bytes read, so a changed file is validated again and the certificate stays by hash. An
-- extern whose type has no law to validate against is not applied (L11.3). A provisional extern
-- is the declared exception: applied without validation, recorded as debt, listed by `check -v`
-- and never certified (4.R5), so its grade is an assumption. A model of `Externs` is the
-- validator of a run: `lawsOf` the laws a declared type states (6.S1, read by the checker),
-- `gradeOf` the grade of the validation of the bytes at a hash (test.lth's `grade` of its verdict
-- and its coverage; `nothing` when refuted or not run), `applied` whether the extern is applied,
-- `identity` that of what it produces. The test it runs is test.lth's, and what the evidence
-- settles is laws.lth's.
Externs : {
  lawsOf : Term -> List String,
  gradeOf : Route -> String -> Maybe Grade,
  applied : Route -> String -> Bool,
  identity : Route -> String -> String,
}
-- settled at the demand's cutoff: the validation's grade is at least the weakest the demand
-- accepts (certification.lth's `accepts`; D-2026-10-14-c: the cutoff is a parameter the demand
-- supplies, never a constant here. The review's W8: whether a sampled validation is enough to
-- apply an extern is the demand's criterion.)
settled (accepts : Grade) (g : Maybe Grade) : Bool :=
  match g { just k -> gradeRank accepts <= gradeRank k ; nothing -> false }
-- applied exactly when its laws are settled at the cutoff, or it is provisional
AppliedWhenSettled (e : Externs) (accepts : Grade) (r : Route) (h : String) : Bool :=
  e.applied r h == (r.provisional or settled accepts (e.gradeOf r h))
-- a type that states no law gives nothing to validate against, so no validation grades the extern
-- and, by `AppliedWhenSettled`, it is not applied (10.S10, L11.3); over the routes that are not
-- provisional, built from their parts
NoLawNoGrade (e : Externs) (n : String) (t : Term) (route h : String) : Bool :=
  not (e.lawsOf t == nil) or e.gradeOf (routeOf n t route false) h == nothing
-- a provisional extern is not validated: its grade is an assumption; over the provisional routes,
-- built from their parts (the review's W4: a premise `not r.provisional or` exempted half the draws)
ProvisionalIsAssumed (e : Externs) (n : String) (t : Term) (route h : String) : Bool :=
  e.gradeOf (routeOf n t route true) h == just AssumedGrade
-- the identity of what it produces carries the hash of what was read: two reads with different
-- hashes are two identities (`hash` itself is the core's, and what it tells apart is its own law)
IdentityCarriesHash (e : Externs) (r : Route) (h k : String) : Bool :=
  h == k or not (e.identity r h == e.identity r k)
Externs := AppliedWhenSettled & NoLawNoGrade & ProvisionalIsAssumed & IdentityCarriesHash

-- A Luthier function passed to a host function crosses as a callback (3.S23; D-2026-10-14-a): the
-- host calls it through the bridge, at the arguments it chooses, and each call gives the
-- function's value there, nothing more than applying it here. The callback takes no token: a
-- function that mentions no `W` holds no effect (10.S1), so the host's calls change no place of
-- the world, and the host's action, with or without a callback, stays one action of the world,
-- described as one (`Described`) and certified along one chain of tokens (§5; D4: the token is
-- the action's, used once by it, and never the callback's). A record whose fields are functions
-- crosses as the record with each field a callback (D-2026-10-14-a): the host reads a field and
-- calls it, and calling the field is calling the function, counted the same. The calls are
-- counted, and the count is in the run's record (12.S18, P2), beside the result: the cost of a
-- fit is in the calls to its objective. [proposed] (D-2026-10-14-a names the count and the record,
-- not its key) The count is per callback crossing, recorded with the route of the extern that
-- received it. A model of `Crossing` is the bridge (run.py): what it gives the host when the host
-- calls at `xs`, the arguments it asked at, with how many calls that was. The theory says nothing
-- of what the host does between the calls; that is the host action's, under `Described`.
Crossing (A B : Concrete) : {
  callBack : (A -> B) -> List A -> List B * Nat,
  callField : List (String * (A -> B)) -> String -> List A -> List B * Nat,
}
mapList (A B : Concrete) (f : A -> B) (xs : List A) : List B :=
  match xs { nil -> nil ; cons x rest -> cons (f x) (mapList A B f rest) }
lengthOf (A : Concrete) (xs : List A) : Nat := match xs { nil -> 0 ; cons x rest -> 1 + lengthOf A rest }
concatFields (A B : Concrete) (xs ys : List (String * (A -> B))) : List (String * (A -> B)) :=
  match xs { nil -> ys ; cons x rest -> cons x (concatFields A B rest ys) }
hasLabel (A B : Concrete) (l : String) (fs : List (String * (A -> B))) : Bool :=
  match fs { nil -> false ; cons (k, g) rest -> k == l or hasLabel A B l rest }
-- each call gives the function's value at the argument: the crossing is faithful
CallbackGivesTheFunction (A B : Concrete) (b : Crossing A B) (f : A -> B) (xs : List A) : Bool :=
  match b.callBack f xs { (ys, n) -> ys == mapList A B f xs }
-- and the calls are counted, one per argument the host asked at (12.S18)
CallsAreCounted (A B : Concrete) (b : Crossing A B) (f : A -> B) (xs : List A) : Bool :=
  match b.callBack f xs { (ys, n) -> n == lengthOf A xs }
-- a record of functions crosses by field: calling a field is calling its function, with its values
-- and its count and no other field's, whatever the other fields and wherever the field sits (the
-- review's W5: over one-field records, a bridge that called the first field whatever the label
-- passed). The first field of a label is the one read, so a label already in `before` is not asked.
RecordCrossesByField (A B : Concrete) (b : Crossing A B) (before after : List (String * (A -> B))) (l : String) (f : A -> B) (xs : List A) : Bool :=
  hasLabel A B l before or b.callField (concatFields A B before (cons (l, f) after)) l xs == b.callBack f xs
Crossing (A B : Concrete) := CallbackGivesTheFunction A B & CallsAreCounted A B & RecordCrossesByField A B

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §4 The actions the first slice needs

-- `print` writes its text as a line at the standard output, a place of the world, and changes no
-- other place; reading a file gives what is at its place, and changes nothing (10.S2; D8: `print`
-- the first action). Stated by their descriptions over the representation; the host's actions in
-- world_model.lut are certified against them by the protocol of §5. `appendStr` is `String`
-- concatenation, the core's `+` on strings [proposed: a body, so that a description runs when a
-- host action is validated against it (§5); declared without one in draft 1, A12 of the naive
-- review, record/IA/review-naive-2026-10-15/].
appendStr (a b : String) : String := a + b
Console (S : Concrete) : {
  world : World S,
  printLine : String -> S -> S * Unit,
  readFile : String -> S -> S * Maybe String,
  stdout : String,
}
lineOf (s : String) : String := appendStr s "\n"
PrintAppendsALine (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (st : S) (s : String) : Bool :=
  c.world.read (stateAfter S Unit (c.printLine s st)) c.stdout
    == just (match c.world.read st c.stdout { nothing -> lineOf s ; just prior -> appendStr prior (lineOf s) })
PrintChangesOnlyStdout (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (st : S) (s p : String) : Bool :=
  p == c.stdout or c.world.read (stateAfter S Unit (c.printLine s st)) p == c.world.read st p
-- [proposed] The standard output is a place and not a file: reading the file at a path gives what
-- is read at that place, for every path other than the standard output's place (refuted at
-- p = "stdout" without the premise, by tests/tester/world_laws.lut: `readFile` reads files, and
-- the representation reads the printed text there).
ReadGivesThePlace (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (st : S) (p : String) : Bool :=
  p == c.stdout or resultOf S (Maybe String) (c.readFile p st) == c.world.read st p
ReadChangesNothing (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (st : S) (p : String) : Bool :=
  stateAfter S (Maybe String) (c.readFile p st) == st
Console (S : Concrete) := PrintAppendsALine S & PrintChangesOnlyStdout S & ReadGivesThePlace S & ReadChangesNothing S

-- The descriptions the host is held to are the console's, built here from its model and never
-- filled by the host's (the review's W1: with the descriptions fields of the host's model, a host
-- whose `print` did nothing, described as doing nothing, passed every law). `print w s` is held to
-- `printLine s` at the standard output's place, `readFile w p` to `readFile p` at `p`, both world
-- actions; `observe w ps` takes and gives the token, so it is an action (10.S3), held to the
-- identity at the places it reads, with no `own`. Their laws as descriptions (`Described`) follow
-- from `Console`'s.
printDescription (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (s : String) : Described S Unit c.world :=
  { describe := c.printLine s, own := false, places := [c.stdout] }
readFileDescription (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (p : String) : Described S (Maybe String) c.world :=
  { describe := c.readFile p, own := false, places := [p] }
observeDescription (S : Concrete) (c : Console S) (ps : List String) : Described S S c.world :=
  { describe := fn st { (st, st) }, own := false, places := ps }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §5 Certifying a host action

-- A host action is certified against the console's description along one chain of tokens
-- (D-2026-10-13-a): observe the representation, act once, observe it again. The protocol is
-- `agreement`, an action (10.S3, 12.S23): it threads the token and gives it back with the case it
-- gathered, the representation before, the action's result and the representation after. The
-- program goes on with that token and takes the result from the case (the review's W2: the
-- protocol returned a bare Bool and the program's `readFile` got no text from it), so the action
-- is applied once (10.S6) and each token is used once (D4). The observation takes the places,
-- since the host cannot read the whole world: a representation stands for the world at the places
-- observed (the coordinator's point on `files`: a read of a file not yet observed looked like a
-- change). What is observed is the action's declared places and the places the certifier adds,
-- `extra`, so that a change where nothing was declared is seen (10.S7). What the host is held to
-- is a `Held`: the action at its arguments, and the console's description of it; each law builds
-- the one it is about, so the law names its action and the runner reads the action from the law
-- (the one action of the model the law projects), as it read it from the laws that took the token.
--
-- The laws of `Host` are predicates over the case (the review's W2: a law that applied the action
-- was neither a definition, 10.S1, nor an action, 10.S3, and dropped the token and the result;
-- GPT's form). None takes the token, and none is to be sampled with a drawn case, which is no
-- observation of anything: the domain of `c` is the cases `agreement` gathers, so a law with a
-- parameter of type `Observed` is run by the certifier alone, as a law with the token was, and
-- `luthier test` does not draw it. Where such a law sits in certification: at the first
-- application of an action in a run, the runner gathers one case with the run's own arguments by
-- `agreement` and evaluates the law on it (10.S10, L11.2); the entry is that case, graded cases,
-- its binding the model, the arguments and the places added, kept by the hash of the model's cone,
-- which holds the host's code (10.S9, 6.S8), so a changed host is validated again. Whether the one
-- case at the first application serves the rest of the run, at other arguments, is Luciano's
-- question (10.S10 says it does today; the review's W10), left as it stands.
Observed (S : Concrete) (A : Concrete) = { before : S, result : A, after : S }
Held (S : Concrete) (A : Concrete) (m : World S) = { act : Action A, described : Described S A m }
held (S : Concrete) (A : Concrete) (m : World S) (act : Action A) (d : Described S A m) : Held S A m :=
  { act := act, described := d }
concatStrs (xs ys : List String) : List String := match xs { nil -> ys ; cons x rest -> cons x (concatStrs rest ys) }

-- The host's side of the console: its actions, routes to the host (world_model.lut), its
-- observation, and the console whose descriptions they are held to (L11.2: an extern is declared
-- over a type whose laws it is validated against; D-2026-10-13-a).
Host (S : Concrete) : {
  console : Console S,
  observe : W -> List String -> W * S,
  print : W -> String -> W * Unit,
  readFile : W -> String -> W * Maybe String,
}
agreement (S : Concrete) (A : Concrete) (m : World S) (o : Host S) (h : Held S A m) (extra : List String) (w0 : W) : W * Observed S A :=
  match o.observe w0 (concatStrs extra h.described.places) { (w1, before) ->
  match h.act w1 { (w2, x) ->
  match o.observe w2 (concatStrs extra h.described.places) { (w3, after) ->
    (w3, { before := before, result := x, after := after }) } } }

-- what is read at each of the places is the same in two states
readsAlike (S : Concrete) (m : World S) (a b : S) (ps : List String) : Bool :=
  match ps { nil -> true ; cons p rest -> m.read a p == m.read b p and readsAlike S m a b rest }
-- the case agrees with the description: the result is the described one, and at each declared
-- place the world after reads as the description says it does (the runner names the places that
-- differ, 10.S10)
agrees (S : Concrete) (A : Concrete) (m : World S) (h : Held S A m) (c : Observed S A) : Bool :=
  match h.described.describe c.before { (described, x) ->
    x == c.result and readsAlike S m described c.after h.described.places }
-- an observation's case: its result reads at the places as the world read before it, and the
-- world reads the same after it. Not `agrees`, whose result is compared whole: the observation
-- gives the world at its places, and the case's `before` holds the places added too.
observes (S : Concrete) (m : World S) (h : Held S S m) (c : Observed S S) : Bool :=
  readsAlike S m c.result c.before h.described.places and readsAlike S m c.before c.after h.described.places
-- the places observed that the action did not declare
undeclared (declared ps : List String) : List String :=
  match ps { nil -> nil ; cons p rest -> if memberStr p declared then undeclared declared rest else cons p (undeclared declared rest) }

-- `HostAgrees`, one law per action: a case of the action at its arguments agrees with the
-- console's description of it. `readFile` at the standard output's place is no file read: the
-- console's `ReadGivesThePlace` says nothing there, and the law exempts it as the console does
-- (the review's M1: the description reads nothing at that place while a host may read a file so
-- named, and a host was refused for a file it was never asked about).
HostAgreesPrint (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (s : String) (c : Observed S Unit) : Bool :=
  agrees S Unit o.console.world (held S Unit o.console.world (fn w { o.print w s }) (printDescription S o.console s)) c
HostAgreesReadFile (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (p : String) (c : Observed S (Maybe String)) : Bool :=
  p == o.console.stdout
    or agrees S (Maybe String) o.console.world (held S (Maybe String) o.console.world (fn w { o.readFile w p }) (readFileDescription S o.console p)) c
-- the observation reads what is at the places and changes nothing there, so that the protocol's
-- own observations are not a change of the world (10.S7); its case is gathered as any action's,
-- observe, observe at `ps`, observe (the review's W1: two equal readings said only that the
-- observer was deterministic)
HostObserveChangesNothing (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (ps : List String) (c : Observed S S) : Bool :=
  observes S o.console.world (held S S o.console.world (fn w { o.observe w ps }) (observeDescription S o.console ps)) c
-- The host's side of 10.S7 (the review's W1: `ChangesOnlyDeclared` held the description to its
-- places, and nothing held the host): across an action, the places observed that it did not
-- declare read after as they read before. `extra` is the certifier's parameter, the places it
-- adds to the observation (those the run has touched, a sample of paths), since `agreement`
-- observes what it is asked and no observation covers the whole world. Over every held action of
-- the host, `h` the one the case was gathered from.
HostChangesOnlyDeclared (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (A : Concrete) (h : Held S A o.console.world) (extra : List String) (c : Observed S A) : Bool :=
  readsAlike S o.console.world c.before c.after (undeclared h.described.places extra)

-- [assumed] The root of trust. Every law above compares the host's observation with the host's
-- observation; none can say that `observe` reads the world rather than the host's own memory (in
-- the specimen, `print` appends to a Python string and `observe` reads that string back; the
-- terminal gets the text from `run` at the end, outside every law). That the observation at a
-- place is what is at the place outside the program is a function of no value Luthier holds
-- (10.S1), so it is an axiom (11.R3: stated, never tested, its obligations graded assumed,
-- listed by `check -v`), and `ObserverIsFaithful` is the law of `Host` that asserts it. Its
-- evidence is a citation (the host's source read and vouched for, 6.S9) or an assumption, graded
-- assumed (6.S10), never a sample; a run that uses the host accepts that grade or is refused, so
-- that its certificate says under what hypothesis it holds (10.S13).
readsTheWorld (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (p : String) : Bool :=
  axiom "the host's observation at a place is what is at the place outside the program"
ObserverIsFaithful (S : Concrete) (o : Host S) (p : String) : Bool := readsTheWorld S o p
Host (S : Concrete) := HostAgreesPrint S & HostAgreesReadFile S & HostObserveChangesNothing S & HostChangesOnlyDeclared S & ObserverIsFaithful S
